[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m \[997x \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m This means that there exists an integer \(k\) such that:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m \[997x = 1000k + 1\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m or equivalently,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m \[997x - 1000k = 1\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m This is a linear Diophantine equation of the form \(ax + by = c\), and it has a solution if and only if \(\gcd(a, b) = 1\). Here, \(\gcd(997, 1000) = 1\), so a solution exists. We can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find the solution.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m Let's implement this in Python.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m ```python
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m def extended_gcd(a, b):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m     if a == 0:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m         return b, 0, 1
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m     gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m     x = y1 - (b // a) * x1
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m     y = x1
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m     return gcd, x, y
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m # Find the modular inverse of 997 modulo 1000
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m a = 997
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m b = 1000
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m gcd, x, y = extended_gcd(a, b)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=2696761)[0m # Since gcd is 1, x is the modular inverse of a modulo b
